<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogImage={ogImage} heroImage={heroImage} heroAlt={heroAlt} heroCaption={heroCaption} schema={schema} publishDate={publishDate} readTime={readTime} category="PNR Tutorial">
  <section>
    <p>Imagine you just booked a flight from Buenos Aires to Madrid for a family of four. You have the PNR on your Amadeus screen, with all the codes, times, and confirmed classes. Now comes the part that steals time from many agents: turning that cryptic text block into a proposal the client can read, understand, and, most importantly, approve without hesitation.</p>

    <p>The good news is that converting a <strong>PNR to PDF</strong> no longer requires Word, Excel, or manual templates. In this article, we show you the exact three-step process, with practical tips so every quote you send sells more and represents your agency as the professional business it is.</p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
      <a href="/precios/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 bg-magenta hover:bg-magenta-500 text-white font-semibold rounded-full transition-colors">Start your 7-day free trial. No credit card. Setup in 24h.</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Step 1: Copy the PNR from your Amadeus terminal</h2>
    <p>The first step is the simplest and most important. Open the booking in Amadeus using the locator and copy the full PNR block. Make sure to include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The passenger name line.</li>
      <li>The flight segments (itinerary).</li>
      <li>The ticketing time limit line (<code>TK TL</code>).</li>
      <li>Phones and reference contacts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The PNR does not need to be perfectly formatted. The important thing is that the block is complete, because the <a href="/en/pnr-converter/">PNR Converter</a> reads and automatically discards information that adds no value to the final client.</p>

    <h3>Practical tip: do not send the raw PNR</h3>
    <p>Sending the PNR exactly as it comes out of Amadeus forces the client to interpret codes like <code>HK</code>, <code>EZE</code>, or <code>MAD</code>. That creates friction, doubts, and often a poor perception of your service. The goal of the PDF is to remove that friction in one go.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Step 2: Paste the PNR into the PNR Converter</h2>
    <p>Once the code is copied, open the free <a href="/en/pnr-converter/">Quotix PNR Converter</a> and paste the text into the input field. Within seconds, the system analyzes the content and extracts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Airlines and flight numbers.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Origin and destination airports.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Departure and arrival dates and times.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Booking classes and status codes.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Passenger names.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>The result is a clean, visual, organized itinerary where each segment is presented with the key information highlighted. You do not have to type anything or worry about transcription errors.</p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
      <a href="/precios/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 border border-slate-300 text-slate-700 hover:border-magenta hover:text-magenta font-semibold rounded-full transition-colors">Ready to quote like agencies that sell seriously? Try Quotix free for 7 days.</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Step 3: Customize, review, and generate the PDF</h2>
    <p>Before downloading the file, review that everything is correct. Check that passenger names are spelled correctly, dates match what was agreed with the client, and no segment is missing.</p>

    <h3>Add your brand and extra services</h3>
    <p>The professional PDF does not only show flights. You can also include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your agency logo and contact details.</li>
      <li>Hotels, transfers, insurance, and tours.</li>
      <li>Commercial terms, payment methods, and cancellation policy.</li>
      <li>The final selling price, with your margins already applied.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This customization is key for the client to perceive value and professionalism. A well-designed quote builds trust before the client even reads the price.</p>

    <h3>Download and send</h3>
    <p>Once ready, click download. The PDF is ready to send by email, WhatsApp, or print. If you use <a href="/funcionalidades/">Quotix</a>, the quote is also automatically saved in the CRM, linked to the client, so you can follow up from one place.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6 tips to make your quote PDF sell more</h2>
    <p>Converting the PNR to PDF is only half the job. The other half is designing a proposal that leads to a sale. Here are some practical tips:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use a clear title:</strong> "Trip quote to Madrid · García Family · October 2026" is better than a generic file.</li>
      <li><strong>Highlight value, not just price:</strong> Include hotel name, included meals, tours, and any differential benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Separate items clearly:</strong> Flights, hotels, transfers, and insurance should be clearly differentiated with their prices.</li>
      <li><strong>Add a note of urgency:</strong> "Subject to availability. Ticketing deadline: October 15."</li>
      <li><strong>Include your contact details:</strong> Let the client know who they are talking to and how to confirm the booking.</li>
      <li><strong>Offer the next action:</strong> End with a button or link that says "Confirm booking" or "Check availability."</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common mistakes when converting PNR to PDF</h2>
    <p>Although the process is simple, there are common mistakes that can cost you a sale. Avoid them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sending the PNR without reviewing:</strong> Always check names and dates.</li>
      <li><strong>Showing net costs:</strong> Make sure the PDF does not include <code>RM</code> lines with internal prices.</li>
      <li><strong>PDFs without brand identity:</strong> A generic file lowers the perceived value of your agency.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting the deadline:</strong> The client needs to know how long they have to confirm.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions about converting PNR to PDF</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>What do I need to convert a PNR to PDF? <span class="text-magenta">▼</span></summary>
